use Libraries.Web.Page.all
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.ComputerScienceStandards2026
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.QuorumStandards

/*
    Tutorial: Themes and Dark Mode, part of the user interface series.
*/
class UserInterfacesThemes is QuorumPage
    // The site's sets of standards, built once for every page.
    QuorumStandards standards

    on create
        SetLocation("tutorials/ui/themes.html")
        SetShortTitle("Themes and Dark Mode")
        SetSectionName("User Interfaces")
    end

    action Setup
        // For search engines and link previews.
        SetDescription("How to use themes in Quorum to switch a form between light mode and dark mode, build your own theme as a class, change the colors of every kind of control, give some controls their own colors, and choose colors with enough contrast to read.")

        Title("Themes and Dark Mode")

        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("Many programs let you choose how they look. "):Strong("Dark mode"):Text(" draws light text
on dark backgrounds, and "):Strong("light mode"):Text(" does the opposite. Some people find
one easier to read than the other, and some need very strong contrast
to read at all. In Quorum, the look of every control comes from one object,
its "):Strong("interface options"):Text(". In this tutorial, you will switch a form to dark
mode, let a person switch while the program runs, build a theme of your
own, change the colors of the newer controls such as combo boxes and
calendars, and give some buttons their own colors.")

        Subsection("Goals")
        Paragraph("You have the following goals for this tutorial:")
        Goals()
        ListItem("Switch a form between light mode and dark mode")
        ListItem("Build your own theme as a class, and use it in a form or a game")
        ListItem("Change the colors of every control of one kind, including combo boxes,
color wells and calendars, by changing the interface options")
        ListItem("Give a group of controls their own colors with a named set of options")
        ListItem("Choose colors with enough contrast for people to read")
    end

    action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("Do you use dark mode on a phone, computer or app? Why or why not? Think
about a time when text on a screen was hard to read, maybe in bright
sunlight or late at night. What made it hard? If you were designing an app,
who might want light mode, who might want dark mode, and who might need
something else?")

        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary", "media/userInterface/Themes/Vocabulary.csv")

        Subsection("New Code to Learn")
        Paragraph("You will be using the following new pieces of code:")
        Table("New Code to Learn", "media/userInterface/Themes/Code.csv")

        Subsection("CSTA Standards")
        Paragraph("This tutorial covers the following "):Link("CSTA standards", "https://csteachers.org/pk12standards/view/"):Text(":")
        ComputerScienceStandards2026 computerScience = standards:GetComputerScience2026()
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_IMPACTS_OF_ALGORITHMS_AND_DESIGN_9)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:MIDDLE_SCHOOL_IMPACTS_OF_ALGORITHMS_AND_DESIGN_9)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_SOFTWARE_DEVELOPMENT_USER_EXPERIENCE_4)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:MIDDLE_SCHOOL_IMPACTS_OF_COMPUTING_SYSTEMS_37)
        StandardsList(computerScience)
    end

    action Explore
        Section("Explore")
        WhereAControlGetsItsColors()
        DarkModeInAForm()
        LettingAPersonChoose()
        YourOwnTheme()
        ColorsForTheNewerControls()
        ChangingTheThemeInAGame()
        NamedOptions()
        Overrides()
        ChoosingColorsPeopleCanRead()
    end

    private action WhereAControlGetsItsColors
        Subsection("Where a control gets its colors")
        Paragraph("When Quorum draws a control, such as a button, it needs many colors: the
background, the text, the border, the color when it has the focus, and the
color when the mouse is over it. It asks for each color in this order:")
        NumberedList()
        ListItem():Strong("The control itself."):Text(" If you set a color on this one control, that
color wins. This is called an "):Strong("override"):Text(".")
        ListItem():Strong("The control's named options."):Text(" Each kind of control has a name, called
its key. Every "):Code("Button"):Text(" uses the options named "):DoubleQuote():Text("Button"):DoubleQuote():Text(", for example. If
those options have the color, it is used.")
        ListItem():Strong("The theme's own colors."):Text(" Anything not found above comes from the
interface options as a whole.")

        Paragraph("The whole set of interface options is the program's "):Strong("theme"):Text(". Quorum comes
with two themes: "):Code("LightModeOptions"):Text(" and "):Code("DarkModeOptions"):Text(". Every game and
every form starts in light mode.")
    end

    private action DarkModeInAForm
        Subsection("Dark mode in a form")
        Paragraph("To show a form in dark mode, call "):Code("SetToDarkMode"):Text(" before "):Code("Display"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/Themes/Main.quorum")

        Paragraph("The theme changes only colors and shapes. A screen reader still reads the
same names and descriptions, and the keys still work the same way.")
    end

    private action LettingAPersonChoose
        Subsection("Letting a person choose")
        Paragraph("The best choice is often to let each person decide. This form has a button
that switches between the two themes. It uses a "):Strong("form behavior"):Text(", a
behavior that can find the form and page it belongs to, as you saw in the
"):Link("Behaviors", "/tutorials/ui/behavior.html"):Text(" tutorial:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/Themes/Main2.quorum")

        Paragraph("Press "):Key("Tab"):Text(" to reach the button, then "):Key("Space"):Text(" or "):Key("Enter"):Text(" to press it. The
status label says which mode is on, in words, and a screen reader user can
press "):Keys("Shift", "Tab"):Text(" to read it. Do not rely on the colors alone to show what
changed.")
    end

    private action YourOwnTheme
        Subsection("Your own theme")
        Paragraph("A theme is a class. To make your own, start from one of Quorum's themes and
change what you need in "):Code("on create"):Text(". This theme starts from dark mode and
makes the buttons black and white, with a yellow focus color that is easy
to spot. Put it in a file named "):Code("HighContrastOptions.quorum"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/Themes/HighContrastOptions.quorum")

        Paragraph("A form starts with light mode or dark mode, so give it your theme once it is
running, from a behavior. This form has a combo box and a button that turns
high contrast on and off:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/Themes/Main3.quorum")

        Paragraph("A new theme is a new object. If you changed colors in the old theme while
the program ran, those changes are not in the new one. Putting your changes
in a class, as here, means they are never lost.")
    end

    private action ColorsForTheNewerControls
        Subsection("Colors for the newer controls")
        text quote = ""
        quote = quote:GetDoubleQuote()
        Paragraph("Combo boxes, sliders, color wells and calendars each have their own key, and you can
give them colors in your theme with actions that take the key as text, such
as "):Code("SetBackgroundColor(" + quote + "ComboBox" + quote + ", color:Black())"):Text(". Here is what each color
means for them:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem():DoubleQuote():Strong("ComboBox"):DoubleQuote():Strong(":"):Text(" the background and border of the field, the foreground
color of the arrow that opens the list, and the focus border. The list
that opens uses the "):DoubleQuote():Text("MenuPanel"):DoubleQuote():Text(" options.")
        ListItem():DoubleQuote():Strong("ComboBoxChoice"):DoubleQuote():Strong(":"):Text(" the mouse-over color of a row in a combo box's open
list. The row picked with the keyboard keeps the list's selection color.")
        ListItem():DoubleQuote():Strong("Slider"):DoubleQuote():Strong(":"):Text(" the background color is the track, the foreground color
is the filled part of the track, the accent color is the thumb and the
border color is the thumb's edge. While the slider has the focus, the
focus border color is the thumb and its halo, and the focus color is the
thumb's edge.")
        ListItem():DoubleQuote():Strong("ColorWell"):DoubleQuote():Strong(":"):Text(" the background, the border (which is also the edge of
the color swatch), and the focus border. The color picker that opens
uses the "):DoubleQuote():Text("MenuPanel"):DoubleQuote():Text(" options, like a combo box's list.")
        ListItem():DoubleQuote():Strong("Calendar"):DoubleQuote():Strong(":"):Text(" the font color is the days' numbers, the foreground color
is dimmed days, the border color is the ring around today, the accent
color fills the chosen day, the focus border is the ring around the day
the keys are on, and the mouse-over color is the circle under the mouse.")

        Paragraph("Spreadsheets use "):DoubleQuote():Text("Spreadsheet"):DoubleQuote():Text(" in the same way.
Add these lines to the end of "):Code("on create"):Text(" in "):Code("HighContrastOptions"):Text(", so the
combo box, color wells and calendars show a yellow ring when they have the
focus too:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/Themes/NewerControls.quorum")
    end

    private action ChangingTheThemeInAGame
        Subsection("Changing the theme in a game")
        Paragraph("In a game, you work with the interface options directly. "):Code("GetInterfaceOptions"):Text("
returns the theme the game is using. Change it "):Strong("before"):Text(" you add your
controls. This game makes every button navy blue with white text and a
thicker border:")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/Themes/Main4.quorum")

        Paragraph():Code("SetButtonBackgroundColor"):Text(" stores the color in the "):DoubleQuote():Text("Button"):DoubleQuote():Text(" named options,
so only buttons change. There are actions like it for most kinds of
control, such as "):Code("SetCheckboxBackgroundColor"):Text(", "):Code("SetTextFieldFontColor"):Text(" and
"):Code("SetLabelFontColor"):Text(". Actions without a control's name, such as
"):Code("SetBackgroundColor(color:Navy())"):Text(", change the theme's own colors, which
every control falls back on. A game can also use your own theme: put
"):Code("HighContrastOptions highContrast"):Text(" and "):Code("SetInterfaceOptions(highContrast)"):Text("
at the start of "):Code("CreateGame"):Text(".")

        BlockEditor("Main.quorum", "html/sources/tutorials/ui/Themes/Main4.quorum", "", true, true)
    end

    private action NamedOptions
        Subsection("Named options: colors for some controls")
        Paragraph("Sometimes a few controls need to look different. A button that deletes
something might be dark red to warn people. You can store your own named
options in the theme, then tell a control to use that name with
"):Code("SetInterfaceOptionsKey"):Text(".")

        Paragraph("A control uses only one name. A button that uses "):DoubleQuote():Text("Warning"):DoubleQuote():Text(" no longer looks
in "):DoubleQuote():Text("Button"):DoubleQuote():Text(", so it would lose the button colors you did not set. To keep
them, start from a copy of the "):DoubleQuote():Text("Button"):DoubleQuote():Text(" options with
"):Code("CopyInterfaceOptions"):Text(". Add these lines to "):Code("CreateGame"):Text(" from the last
program, before "):Code("Add(panel)"):Text(":")
        CodeExampleFromFile("sources/tutorials/ui/Themes/WarningButton.quorum")

        Paragraph("Put the button in the "):Key("Tab"):Text(" order too, between "):Code("quit"):Text(" and "):Code("play"):Text(". Notice
that the button's name says what it does. Color alone cannot warn someone
who cannot see it, or who sees red and green the same way. The name and
description warn everyone.")
    end

    private action Overrides
        Subsection("Overrides: one control at a time")
        Paragraph("You can also set a color on a single control, such as
"):Code("play:SetBackgroundColor(color:Green())"):Text(". This override wins over every
theme, so the button stays green in light mode and dark mode. A green
button with white text may be easy to read in one theme and hard in the
other. Use overrides only when a color must never change.")
    end

    private action ChoosingColorsPeopleCanRead
        Subsection("Choosing colors people can read")
        Paragraph("A theme is only good if people can read it. Text needs enough "):Strong("contrast"):Text("
with the color behind it. The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines ask for a
contrast ratio of at least 4.5 to 1 for normal text. Quorum's own light
mode was chosen with this in mind: even the dimmed days of a calendar keep
4.5 to 1 against the calendar's background. You can learn how to measure
contrast in "):Link("Luminance and Color Contrast", "/tutorials/accessibility/luminanceandcolorcontrast.html"):Text(", and how
to choose colors in "):Link("Color in Application", "/tutorials/accessibility/colorInApplication.html"):Text(".")

        Paragraph("Some people need larger controls as well as stronger colors. A game can
make every control bigger with "):Code("SetInterfaceScale"):Text(". For example,
"):Code("SetInterfaceScale(1.5)"):Text(" makes controls one and a half times their size.")
    end

    action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("In this activity, you and a partner design a theme for a real kind of user,
and check that it's easy to read and to use with the keyboard. The goal is
to change a theme's colors on purpose, for a reason, and to measure whether
text stands out enough from its background. Start by choosing who your
theme is for, such as someone who reads their phone outside in sunlight,
someone who uses a computer late at night, or someone with low vision. Each
partner then writes a class that starts from "):Code("LightModeOptions"):Text(" or
"):Code("DarkModeOptions"):Text(" and changes at least four colors, including the focus
colors, so a keyboard user can always see where they are.")

        Paragraph("Next, make a form with at least three controls, including a combo box or a
calendar, each with a name and a description, and add a button that
switches to your theme. Measure the contrast of your text and background
colors with a contrast checker, and change any pair below 4.5 to 1. Finally,
trade programs and use only the keyboard to reach each control. Can you
always tell which control has the focus? Which design principles did your
partner's theme follow, and which did it miss?")
    end

    action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("Themes let one change reach every control in a program. Which of your
colors did you have to change after checking contrast? Why might a program
offer more than two themes? What else, besides color, could a person want
to choose about how a program looks?")
    end
end
